Q: program to change volume of an audio file segmentation fault A: <p>In analyzing this issue and trying out your program, indeed, I received a segmentation fault.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/SoundVolume/bin/Release$ ./SoundVolume aircraft056.wav jet.wav 1.02
Segmentation fault (core dumped)
</code></pre>
<p>As noted in the good comments, the culprit here is the referencing of the address of the pointer to the sample variable and not the address of the sample variable.</p>
<pre><code>    *buffer *= factor;
    fwrite(&amp;buffer, sizeof(buffer), 1, output);
</code></pre>
<p>And as also noted in the good comments, processing the wave file is not just a matter of reading forty-four bytes at a time, applying the scaling factor, and then writing out the new value to the output file.  If one goes out to the web and searches on the description and format of a wave file, it is basically divided into two sections.  The first forty-four bytes contain header information that you would want to copy to the new output file.  Within that header information, one would want to acquire information on the sample size to use.  The second section contains all of the sampling data which would need to be read,  have the scaling factor applied, and then write out the scaled value to the output file.</p>
<p>With that, following is a refactored version of the code owing in part to examples and information on wave files readily available on the web.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;

typedef struct headroom
{
    char ID[4];
    int32_t file_size;
    char format[4];
    char subchunk_1_ID[4];
    int32_t subchunk_1_sz;
    int16_t audio_format;
    int16_t channels;
    int32_t sample_rate;
    int32_t byte_rate;
    int16_t block_align;
    int16_t bits_per_sample;
    char subchunk_2_ID[4];
    int32_t subchunk_2_sz;
} headroom;

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    headroom wave;                          /* Structure to hold header data*/
    int8_t  sample1;                        /* One byte sample values       */
    int16_t sample2;                        /* Two byte sample values       */
    int32_t sample3;                        /* Four byte sample values      */

    if (argc != 4)                          /* Check command line arguments */
    {
        printf(&quot;Usage: ./volume input.wav output.wav factor\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }

    FILE *input = fopen(argv[1], &quot;rb&quot;);     /* Open wav file for reading    */
    if (input == NULL)
    {
        printf(&quot;Could not open file.\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }

    FILE *output = fopen(argv[2], &quot;wb&quot;);    /* Open target file for writing */
    if (output == NULL)
    {
        printf(&quot;Could not open file.\n&quot;);
        return 1;
    }

    float factor = atof(argv[3]);           /* Derive scaling factor        */

    /* TODO: Copy header from input file to output file                     */

    if (fread(&amp;wave, sizeof(wave), 1, input) == 1)
        fwrite(&amp;wave, sizeof(wave), 1, output);
    else
    {
        fclose(input);
        fclose(output);
        return 1;
    }

    printf(&quot;Statistics\n=========================================\n&quot;);
    printf(&quot;ID..............: %c%c%c%c\n&quot;, wave.ID[0], wave.ID[1], wave.ID[2], wave.ID[3]);
    printf(&quot;File size.......: %d\n&quot;, wave.file_size);
    printf(&quot;Format..........: %c%c%c%c\n&quot;, wave.format[0], wave.format[1], wave.format[2], wave.format[3]);
    printf(&quot;Sub Chunk #1....: %c%c%c%c\n&quot;, wave.subchunk_1_ID[0], wave.subchunk_1_ID[1], wave.subchunk_1_ID[2], wave.subchunk_1_ID[3]);
    printf(&quot;Size #1.........: %d\n&quot;, wave.subchunk_1_sz);
    printf(&quot;Audio format....: %d\n&quot;, wave.audio_format);
    printf(&quot;Audio chls......: %d\n&quot;, wave.channels);
    printf(&quot;Sample rate.....: %d\n&quot;, wave.sample_rate);
    printf(&quot;Byte rate.......: %d\n&quot;, wave.byte_rate);
    printf(&quot;Block align.....: %d\n&quot;, wave.block_align);
    printf(&quot;Bits per sample.: %d\n&quot;, wave.bits_per_sample);
    printf(&quot;Sub Chunk #2....: %c%c%c%c\n&quot;, wave.subchunk_2_ID[0], wave.subchunk_2_ID[1], wave.subchunk_2_ID[2], wave.subchunk_2_ID[3]);
    printf(&quot;Size #2.........: %d\n&quot;, wave.subchunk_2_sz);

    /* Now read two bytes at a time and apply the volume factor             */

    if (wave.bits_per_sample == 8)
    {
        while(fread(&amp;sample1, sizeof(int8_t), 1, input) == 1)
        {
            sample1 = (int8_t) (sample1 * factor);
            fwrite(&amp;sample1, sizeof(int8_t), 1, output);
        }
    }
    else if (wave.bits_per_sample == 16)
    {
        while(fread(&amp;sample2, sizeof(int16_t), 1, input) == 1)
        {
            sample2 = (int16_t) (sample2 * factor);
            fwrite(&amp;sample2, sizeof(int16_t), 1, output);
        }
    }
    else if (wave.bits_per_sample == 32)
    {
        while(fread(&amp;sample3, sizeof(int32_t), 1, input) == 1)
        {
            sample3 = (int32_t) (sample3 * factor);
            fwrite(&amp;sample3, sizeof(int32_t), 1, output);
        }
    }
    else
    {
        printf(&quot;Currently do not support bits per sample\n&quot;);
    }

    // Close files
    fclose(input);
    fclose(output);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Testing out that code with a sample wave file produced various volume changes.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/SoundVolume/bin/Release$ ./SoundVolume aircraft056.wav jet.wav 1.00
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/SoundVolume/bin/Release$ ./SoundVolume aircraft056.wav jet.wav 1.02
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/SoundVolume/bin/Release$ ./SoundVolume aircraft056.wav jet.wav 1.10
</code></pre>
<p>As one would expect, applying a value of 1.00 resulted in a copy of the wave file at the same volume.  Testing out other factors did indeed change the volume level.</p>
<p>The takeaway from this issue would be to delve further into the format and structure of audio files to understand how aspects of the file could properly be changed as well as delving into additional C program tutorials as it pertains to reading and writing to files along with the proper use of pointers and address references.</p>
